    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I need to prep a quick brief for our CTO about our scaling strategy. Can you give me a high-level summary of the differences between vertical scaling and horizontal scaling? I need to explain it to someone who understands the business side but isn't a devops expert, so keep it professional but clear.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's a brief summary: \n\nVertical scaling involves increasing the power and capabilities of individual servers or instances within your infrastructure, typically by adding more CPU, memory, or storage. This approach is like upgrading a car's engine - it meets the increasing demands within the existing framework. \n\nOn the other hand, horizontal scaling involves adding more servers or instances to complement the existing infrastructure, effectively creating a larger, more flexible system. This is like buying multiple cars - it can handle the increased load, but it doesn't necessarily upgrade the capabilities of the individual vehicles.",
